<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  Polkit actions for the SuperManager daemon.

  `supermgrd` runs as root on the system bus and its bus policy lets any
  local user send it messages, because the unprivileged GUI has to be able
  to reach it. Polkit is what decides whether a given caller may perform a
  given action once the message arrives.

  Install to /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/. The action ids here must match
  the constants in supermgrd/src/polkit.rs — there is a test that checks
  they do, because a missing rule is an implicit deny that reads like a bug.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>SuperManager</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/franzjeger/SuperManager</vendor_url>

  <action id="org.supermgr.daemon.secrets">
    <description>Read stored SSH keys, passwords and backups</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to read SuperManager's stored credentials</message>

    <!--
      auth_admin, not auth_admin_keep: each grant discloses long-lived
      credentials — a private key or a host password — so one
      authentication must not silently cover later reads.

      allow_any and allow_inactive are both no. Reading the credential
      store is for someone sitting at an active local session, not a
      remote or switched-away one.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="org.supermgr.daemon.ssh-connect">
    <description>Open an SSH session to a managed host</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to open an SSH session with SuperManager's stored credentials</message>

    <!--
      auth_admin_keep, not auth_admin. This method stages the host's
      password or private key on disk for the caller's ssh to read, so
      by what it discloses it belongs with the secrets action above.

      It is also the Connect button. auth_admin would mean a password
      prompt for every SSH session an operator opens, and a control that
      makes the tool unusable gets switched off — a policy file deleted
      in irritation protects nothing. The grace period is a real
      concession, not a formality: within it, a second caller on the same
      session is not asked again.

      Change it to auth_admin if your threat model needs that; nothing in
      the daemon depends on the weaker setting.

      allow_any and allow_inactive stay no, as for secrets. Opening a
      session with stored credentials is for someone at an active local
      seat, not a remote or switched-away one.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

  <action id="org.supermgr.daemon.tailscale-exit-node">
    <description>Route this machine's traffic through a Tailscale exit node</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to send this machine's network traffic through another device</message>

    <!--
      Gated even though starting a VPN profile is not, and the asymmetry is
      deliberate.

      The bus policy for this daemon allows context="default", i.e. every
      local account. An exit node sends every packet this machine emits
      through a host the caller picked. Ungated, any local user could
      silently redirect another user's traffic through a machine they
      control — which is a different thing from starting a VPN an admin
      already configured and stored credentials for.

      auth_admin_keep, not auth_admin: choosing between exit nodes means
      trying one, checking it, trying another. A prompt per attempt is the
      kind of friction that ends with the policy file deleted.

      Clearing the exit node goes through the same action. Being asked to
      authenticate to stop routing through somebody else is mildly annoying
      and the alternative is worse: an ungated clear is a way to knock a
      machine off its exit node without asking anyone.
    -->
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
